General information for Version u235ib46o28t6DNcnu5ef0ST25q       9228 1451  105
                                                                  9228 1451  106
- The basis is the Empire calculation 'ib50' (with capture at the 9228 1451  107
  upper limit of Jandel data, tuned to better fit Wallner and     9228 1451  108
  Allmond data). The model parameters are tuned to improve        9228 1451  109
  agreement with Kammerdiener ddx at 14 MeV.                      9228 1451  110
  Non-threshold reactions e.g. (n,a), (n,x) are extrapolated      9228 1451  111
  down to thermal energies as 1/v.                                9228 1451  112
- Resonance parameters are ORNL version:                          9228 1451  113
ECHO is off.                                                      9228 1451  114
- Prompt nu-bar near thermal energies is evaluated by Pronyaev,   9228 1451  115
  The prompt nu-bar (nu_p) values follow Reed data taken from the 9228 1451  116
  EXFOR extry 10427003 up to 26.4 eV. Simon and Frehaut data      9228 1451  117
  shapes are identical; they were normalized to Reed data in the  9228 1451  118
  interval 10-27 eV; Simon data were adopted from 30 eV to 75 eV. 9228 1451  119
  Subsequently, all nubar data were renormalized to the thermal   9228 1451  120
  value of 2.41161, which agrees with Standards-2017 within       9228 1451  121
  quoted uncertainties.                                           9228 1451  122
    The energy grid of Reed data is modified slightly by shifting 9228 1451  123
  the energies at 1.135 eV to 1.5 eV and 2.370 eV to 2.25 eV      9228 1451  124
  decreasing slightly the nu_p value, at 2.04 eV to 2.36,         9228 1451  125
  thus narrowing the dip order to prevent a positive trend in     9228 1451  126
  reactivity with above-thermal leakage fraction in thermal       9228 1451  127
  solution systems.                                               9228 1451  128
    The nu_prompt in the range 30-70 keV is reduced, similarly    9228 1451  129
  like in the JENDL-4.0 evaluation.                               9228 1451  130
    Additionally, the revised interpretation of Standards-2017    9228 1451  131
  (Jul.2017) caused an increase in the fission cross section      9228 1451  132
  in the URR, which is compensated by a decrease in nu_prompt     9228 1451  133
  starting at 100 eV, reaching the minimum value at 20 keV.       9228 1451  134
  The nu_prompt is also reduced by up to 0.28 in the energy       9228 1451  135
  range 1.2-2.4 MeV to improve the performance in integral        9228 1451  136
  benchmarks. This is thought to counteract the adjustment done   9228 1451  137
  to nu_p in ENDF/B-VII.1, but the details are not known.         9228 1451  138
    At higher energies the evaluation by D. Neudecker is adopted. 9228 1451  139
  (Private communication, D. Neudecker, 24 Feb. 2022              9228 1451  140
  File: "U235nubar_tweak_LA-UR-22-21516.endf.txt").               9228 1451  141
                                                                  9228 1451  142
- Fission cross section is consistent with Standards-2017.        9228 1451  143
  Above the resolved resonance range several high-resolution      9228 1451  144
  measurements are available, which show very good consistency,   9228 1451  145
  but the structure is not represented in earlier evaluations.    9228 1451  146
  The fission cross section with fluctuations was provided by     9228 1451  147
  I. Duran (private communication, Sept.2017), renormalized       9228 1451  148
  over broad energy ranges to agree with Standards-2017.          9228 1451  149
- Other fission data (except PFNS and cross sections) are         9228 1451  150
  from ENDF/B-VII.1                                               9228 1451  151
- Delayed nu-bar and spectra are taken from ENDF/B-VII.1          9228 1451  152
- PFNS is as provided by D. Neudecker (private communication,     9228 1451  153
  file "VIII1beta1_MF31MT452455456_LA-UR-23-20728.endf",          9228 1451  154
  3 Feb. 2023).                                                   9228 1451  155
- Covariances of the cross sections in the resonance region       9228 1451  156
  were determined from the covariances of the resonance           9228 1451  157
  parameters because the latter is impractical due to the         9228 1451  158
  large size (1.7 MB). The reactions cross sections with          9228 1451  159
  covariances include elastic, fission, capture and the           9228 1451  160
  corresponding cross-reaction covariances.                       9228 1451  161
  The total is defined as the sum of the partials.                9228 1451  162
- The covariance matrix prior for the cross sections and          9228 1451  163
  Legendre coefficients of the angular distributions above        9228 1451  164
  the resolved resonance range is generated using the Monte       9228 1451  165
  Carlo technique by statistical sampling of the model            9228 1451  166
  parameter in the Empire calculations. About 3000 ENDF           9228 1451  167
  files were generated in the process. The first discrete         9228 1451  168
  inelastic cross section is treated explicitly while all         9228 1451  169
  the others are lumped into MT 851. Similarly, absorption        9228 1451  170
  reactions MT649 (m,p) and the MT800 series discrete level       9228 1451  171
  (n,alpha) are lumped into MT 852. This is necessary to          9228 1451  172
  preserve unitarity without defining the covariances for         9228 1451  173
  each reaction channel separately.                               9228 1451  174
- The final covariance matrices for all reactions (including      9228 1451  175
  cross reaction correlations) were obtained by the generalised   9228 1451  176
  least-squares method in the GANDR system using measured         9228 1451  177
  data from the EXFOR data base, except for fission,              9228 1451  178
  in which case the Standards-2017 was input to GANDR             9228 1451  179
  instead of the experimental data. A fully-correlated            9228 1451  180
  uncertainty of 1.2 percent was included to account for the      9228 1451  181
  unrecognised sources of uncertainty.                            9228 1451  182
  Experimental data marked with * were renormalized to the        9228 1451  183
  IRDFF-1.05 cross sections @ https://www-nds.iaea.org/IRDFF/.    9228 1451  184
  Total cross section                                             9228 1451  185
  Selected data for fitting the total were the following:         9228 1451  186
       Author               Year  EXFOR entry & subentry          9228 1451  187
    1. C.A.UTTLEY,ET.AL. (66)     X4 21088  4 (60<E<960 keV)      9228 1451  188
    2. V.P.VERTEBNYY,ET.AL. (80)  X4 40609  2 E=24.5 keV          9228 1451  189
    3. W.P.POENITZ,ET.AL. (81)    X4 10935  6 E>200 keV           9228 1451  190
    4. J.Cabe,ET.AL. (73)         X4 20480 17                     9228 1451  191
    6. R.B.Schwartz,ET.AL. (74)   X4 10280  5 *2                  9228 1451  192
    7. F.L.Green,ET.AL. (73)      X4 10588  2                     9228 1451  193
    8. J.Cabe,ET.AL. (73)         X4 20480 18                     9228 1451  194
