                                                                  9228 1451  198
   Capture cross section                                          9228 1451  199
   Capture cross sections in EXFOR were measured mainly as        9228 1451  200
   ratios. At present we could not include them in the            9228 1451  201
   analysis. the Gwin data (EXFOR entry 14149003) are             9228 1451  202
   labelled as "derived" and do not have the uncertainties.       9228 1451  203
   Selected data for fitting were only:                           9228 1451  204
       M.Jandel,ET.AL.      (12)  X4 14149  3  2<E<60 keV         9228 1451  205
                                                                  9228 1451  206
  (n,2n)                                                          9228 1451  207
  The EXFOR entry #21568004 by J. Frehaut, which includes the     9228 1451  208
  normalisation correction for new Standards, was selected.       9228 1451  209
  Furthermore the data points below 8.5 MeV were rejected         9228 1451  210
  because of experimental uncertainties due to the difficulty     9228 1451  211
  in discriminating between neutrons from the (n,2n) and the      9228 1451  212
  fission reaction.                                               9228 1451  213
  The measurement #20795010 by D.S. Mather was rejected due       9228 1451  214
  to unreasonably large discrepancy with the Frehaut data         9228 1451  215
  and the shape, which was in contradiction with the model        9228 1451  216
  calculation.                                                    9228 1451  217
  Hence, selected data for fitting (n,2n) were only:              9228 1451  218
      J.Frehaut          (2016) X4 entry 21568004  E>8 MeV        9228 1451  219
                                                                  9228 1451  220
  (n,3n)                                                          9228 1451  221
  There are only two data sets in EXFOR. Only the data in the     9228 1451  222
  EXFOR entry 10795002 by Veeser were adopted, while the data     9228 1451  223
  in the EXFOR entry #20795011 by Mather were rejected.           9228 1451  224
  Hence, selected data for fitting (n,3n) were only:              9228 1451  225
      L.R.Veeser,ET.AL.    (78)  X4 10795  2                      9228 1451  226
                                                                  9228 1451  227
- Cross-material covariances were prepared manually from the      9228 1451  228
  Standards-2017 file. Unrecognised sources of uncertainties      9228 1451  229
  (USU) of 1.2 percent were added to the cross-covariances of     9228 1451  230
  the fission cross sections between different materials.         9228 1451  231
                                                                  9228 1451  232
- The uncertainties of the delayed neutron multiplicities were    9228 1451  233
  taken from Keepin rather than Tuttle, but taken from the        9228 1451  234
  publication by Tuttle, which includes a comparative analysis:   9228 1451  235
  R. J. Tuttle (1975) Delayed-Neutron Data for Reactor-Physics    9228 1451  236
  Analysis, Nuclear Science and Engineering, 56:1, 37-71,         9228 1451  237
  DOI: 10.13182/NSE75-A26620                                      9228 1451  238
                                                                  9228 1451  239
- The initial covariances for the prompt fission neutron          9228 1451  240
  multiplicities were taken from ENDF/B-VII.1, which originated   9228 1451  241
  from P. Young least-square fit of available nubar data.         9228 1451  242
  The following modifications were made:                          9228 1451  243
  * Below 0.7 eV an additional uncertainty component was added    9228 1451  244
    so as to make the uncertainty match the raw value             9228 1451  245
    recommended by Standards-2017.                                9228 1451  246
  * Between 0.7 eV and 73 eV the uncertainties were essentially   9228 1451  247
    taken from the experimental data, assuming they were          9228 1451  248
    statistical. In the range 5.6 eV to 75 eV an additional       9228 1451  249
    fully correlated component of 0.5 percent was assigned        9228 1451  250
    to account for the systematic uncertainty.                    9228 1451  251
  * Above 73 eV up to 20 keV no data experimental exist, hence    9228 1451  252
    a 2 percent uncertainty was assigned over energy subregions   9228 1451  253
    73, 100, 200, 400, 1000, 2000, 4000, 10000 and 20000 eV.      9228 1451  254
  * An additional 0.4 percent fully correlated uncertainty was    9228 1451  255
    added over the entire energy range from 1.E-5 eV to 30 MeV    9228 1451  256
    to account for the unrecognized systematic uncertaities of    9228 1451  257
    nubar measurements as recommended in Standards 2017.          9228 1451  258
  * Cross-material covariances are given for the nu-bar of        9228 1451  259
    U-233, Pu-239 and Pu-241 as evaluated with Standards-2017.    9228 1451  260
    The covariances are assumed energy-independent, although      9228 1451  261
    the values in Standards-2017 are applicable to the thermal    9228 1451  262
    energy region only.                                           9228 1451  263
                                                                  9228 1451  264
- The covariances of the Legendre elastic scattering component    9228 1451  265
  up to order P2 with cross-correlations as obtained in the       9228 1451  266
  prior are available in MF34.                                    9228 1451  267
                                                                  9228 1451  268
- The PFNS covariances were evaluated with the spectra. In the    9228 1451  269
  thermal range they correspond to Standards-2017. At higher      9228 1451  270
  energies the covariance evaluation was done at LANL together    9228 1451  271
  with the evaluation of the spectra.                             9228 1451  272
